      "title": "Benefit triggers",
      "narration": "Benefits begin when a licensed health professional certifies that the insured meets a trigger. Under the federal tax-qualified standard, the insured must be unable to perform at least two of six activities of daily living, bathing, dressing, eating, toileting, transferring and continence, without substantial assistance, for a period expected to last at least 90 days, or must need substantial supervision because of a severe cognitive impairment such as Alzheimer’s disease. Maryland forbids triggers stricter than these and forbids requiring a prior hospital stay. Gloria’s inability to bathe and dress herself would qualify; difficulty with housework alone would not.",
