      "title": "Levels and settings of care",
      "narration": "Policies describe care by level. Skilled care is daily nursing or rehabilitation ordered by a physician and delivered by licensed professionals. Intermediate care is occasional skilled care, a few times a week. Custodial care is help with daily activities by trained aides, not nurses, and is what most long-term care claims involve. Settings vary: a nursing home, an assisted living facility, home health care in the insured’s own home, adult day care at a center during working hours, and respite care that temporarily relieves a family caregiver. Modern comprehensive policies cover all levels in all settings; older facility-only policies did not.",
